OL,3,CPerseid meteor shower to peak hours
OL,4,Cafter solar eclipse: When and how to
OL,5,Cwatch
OL,7,FThe Perseids are created by debris
OL,8,Fleft behind by the comet called Swift-
OL,9,FTuttle.
OL,11,FAs the comet gets closer to the Sun,
OL,12,Fit heats up and sheds pieces of
OL,13,Fmaterial, according to the Royal
OL,14,FMuseums Greenwich (RMG).
OL,16,FWhen Earth passes through this debris
OL,17,Fas it travels around the Sun, the
OL,18,Fparticles enter our atmosphere at
OL,19,Fspeeds between seven and 45 miles per
OL,20,Fsecond.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G2/5
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,309,300,200,140,000,100
PN,30803
SC,0003
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F308A1786520878
OL,3,CPerseid meteor shower to peak hours
OL,4,Cafter solar eclipse: When and how to
OL,5,Cwatch
OL,7,FThe average Perseid meteor travels at
OL,8,Faround 36 miles per second.
OL,10,FRMG says that as the particles enter
OL,11,Fthe atmosphere, the air in front of
OL,12,Fthem is compressed and heated to
OL,13,Fthousands of degrees Celsius.
OL,15,FSmaller particles vaporise, creating
OL,16,Fthe bright streaks of light seen from
OL,17,FEarth, while larger pieces can produce
OL,18,Fbright fireballs.
OL,20,FWhen can you see the shower?
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G3/5
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
